export const AUTHORING_GUIDE_MARKDOWN = "# RPGCode program authoring\n\n**TOOL-10 Program editing (story 11.7, #1705; editor-features T-40).** *In plain terms:* an author can open a `.prg`, see it coloured as they type, jump to its functions, labels and comments, see the shape of the command they are typing, insert a command from a list, start a new program from a header the game chooses, and hide everything but the text. It works the same in the browser editor (which shows the text in Monaco, [ADR 0021](../../adr/0021-monaco-code-editor.md)) and in VS Code (which shows it in its own text editor). None of it needs a syntax grammar from the later grammar epic, and if the language server fails the author can still type and save. *Precisely:* this entry fixes the pieces below; each is shared by both hosts where the host has the part, and no host keeps its own copy of the rules.\n- **Navigation (bookmarks).** `textDocument/documentSymbol` lists, in source order and as one flat list, the functions, classes, labels and comments of the open file. A **comment bookmark** is a comment that starts its line (the first non-blank text is `//`, `**`, `*` or `/*`); its name is the comment's text without the marker and without a closing `*/`, trimmed, and a comment with no letter or digit in it (a ruled line of `-----`) is not listed. A trailing comment after code is not listed. A block comment is listed once, by its first line. Symbols are reported with kind `Function`, `Class`, `Key` (a label) and `String` (a comment). The browser editor shows them as its bookmark list and VS Code shows them in the Outline.\n- **Call tips** are [the signature help above](#tool-1); the browser editor shows them in Monaco's parameter hints, VS Code in its own.\n- **Command reference and insertion.** The quick reference is a projection of the registered commands' documentation (`commandDocumentation`), computed by `editor-model` from the same catalogue as hover, and so needs no running language server. One entry per public name and alias: `name`, `canonicalName`, `aliasOf` (or `null`), `syntax` (the call as it is written, such as `mwin(text)`, with an optional parameter in brackets, a by-reference parameter prefixed `&` and a variadic one ending `...`), the one-sentence `summary`, and `groups`. **Insert** puts `name(` and the names of the **required** parameters, separated by `, `, and `)` at the cursor, replacing any selection; the editor selects the parameter text so the author overtypes it. A command with no required parameters inserts `name()` with the cursor between the brackets. Double-clicking an entry (the browser) or choosing it in the picker (VS Code) inserts it. Insertion is one undoable edit.\n- **Header template.** The preference `program.headerTemplate` (a string, user or project layer, at most 500 characters) is the text a new program starts with. `{file}` becomes the file name (`intro.prg`), `{name}` the file name without its extension, and `{path}` the project-relative path; any other `{…}` is left as written, so a template can never fail. The default is `// {file}` followed by a blank line. **New program** expands the template and opens the created file. It refuses a selected path when its case-insensitive existence check detects an existing file, with an “already exists” message, and creates nothing. A host **MUST** use a genuine atomic exclusive-create operation when its API supports one. The current browser and VS Code adapters instead check the directory listing and then call an ordinary write: an external actor can create the path between those steps, and that write may replace the concurrent file. This disclosed check/write race is accepted for those hosts; it is not an absolute no-overwrite guarantee. See [ADR 0035](../../adr/0035-program-new-no-overwrite-limits.md).\n- **Distraction-free mode** (the legacy \"Notepad mode\"). A command that hides the editor's panels, tab strip and toolbars and leaves only the text, and that a second use or Escape reverses. The browser editor hides its shell panels and the program toolbar; VS Code uses its Zen mode. Neither mode changes the document, the dirty state or the language service.\n- **Failure.** The language server is a helper to the text, never its owner. If it is missing, fails to start, stops, or answers an error, the document still edits, undoes, saves and reopens; the editor says in one status line that language features are unavailable, and the features that need the server (colouring, bookmarks, call tips, hover, diagnostics) simply show nothing. Header insertion, command insertion, undo and save do not use the server.\n- **VS Code extensions.** The language client stays in `extensions/rpgcode` ([TOOL-2](#tool-2)); this entry is the editor epic's answer to its question whether the editor host takes the client over, and the answer is no: two extensions keep two jobs. `extensions/rpgcode` turns semantic highlighting on for the `rpgcode` language and is otherwise unchanged. The editor extension (`tk-reawakened.editor`) adds the commands **New program**, **Insert header**, **Insert command** (a picker over the quick reference) and **Distraction-free mode**; they read the shared preferences and the shared command catalogue, never the language server, so they work when it is absent. The settings of the header template live with the other editor preferences.\n\nThis guide is drawn from the repository's TOOL-10 editor specification. It is a maintained authoring reference, not the legacy HTML manual.";
export const SAVING_HELP_MARKDOWN = "# Saving and external file changes\n\nUse **Save active editor** or **Ctrl+S** (**Cmd+S** on macOS) to save the editor you are working in. **Save All Open Documents** saves all open documents with unsaved changes. The editor shows save failures and conflicts where you can resolve them.\n\n## Changes made by another program\n\nSome hosts can watch project files and report changes while you work. When file watching is unavailable, external changes are checked when you save instead.\n\nBefore writing, the editor compares the file with the version it last read or saved. If it finds a change, it stops the save and shows a conflict. Your edits remain available while you choose how to resolve it. The choices depend on the host: keeping your edits and reloading the disk version are offered when supported.\n\nThe comparison and write are separate operations. A change another program makes after the comparison but before the write can still be replaced. This is a [known save limitation](https://github.com/tk-reawakened/rpgtoolkit-reawakened/issues/2207), not a guarantee that simultaneous writes are safe.\n";
export const LIGHTS_SHADING_HELP_MARKDOWN = "# Lights and shading on a board\n\nShading and lights change the colour of the tiles on a board. Use them to make a room warm, a cave dark, or a sunset glow, without drawing new art. Open a board and use the **Lights and shading** section.\n\n## Shading\n\nShading tints each board cell. Every cell holds three whole numbers, red, green and blue, each from −255 to 255. A positive number brightens that colour and a negative number darkens it. 0 changes nothing. A board keeps one shading layer, and it tints the layer it is cast onto and every layer below it.\n\nChoose **Paint shading on the board**, then a tool:\n\n- **Draw** paints one cell per click or drag. A right-click erases.\n- **Erase** sets cells to 0, 0, 0.\n- **Rectangle** draws an outline between two corner cells. Hold Shift to fill it. It counts cells, so on an isometric board it is not a rectangle on screen.\n- **Flood fill** replaces the connected area. Red, green and blue each flood on their own, so where the channels differ the areas can differ.\n- **Dropper** copies a cell's shade into the brush and changes nothing in the board.\n\nSet the brush with the Shade red, green and blue fields. **Shade grey** sets all three together, and **Zero RGB** sets them to 0. The brush starts at 64, 64, 64, and there is no brush size. Values you type are kept between −255 and 255.\n\nFrom the keyboard, move over the cell grid with the arrow keys and press Space to use the tool on that cell. Rectangle takes two Spaces, one on each corner, and Escape cancels it. **Shade cell** with the column and row fields shades one cell by number.\n\nOne drag, fill or Space is one edit, and one Undo takes it back.\n\n## Lights\n\nA light adds colour to the cells it reaches when the board loads. **Add spotlight** and **Add gradient** place a new light in the middle of the board. A light stays editable until you convert it.\n\n- **Spotlight:** a soft oval. Its middle node is the centre and the other two, Radius point 1 and Radius point 2, are points on its edge. The longer radius sets the angle of the oval and the shorter one its width. The edge and everything beyond it get no light. A radius of 0 lights nothing.\n- **Gradient:** colour fades from the start node to the end node, and the end colours continue beyond them.\n- **Clipped gradient:** the same fade, but only between two lines at right angles to the axis, one through each node. Nothing is lit outside them.\n\nEach light has two shades. For a spotlight they are the shade at the edge and at the centre, and for a gradient the shade at the start and at the end. The fade is a straight line between them. A new light's second shade is the current brush and its first is 0, so it fades to nothing.\n\nDrag a light's numbered dots on the board after choosing **Move lights on the board**, or type node X and Y positions in board pixels. Positions are whole pixels with the top-left of the board at 0, 0.\n\nExactly how it is worked out: each board cell is tested at its top-left corner, `((column − 1) × 32, (row − 1) × 32)` on a normal board and at the centre of its diamond on an isometric one. The light's value there is added to the cell's painted shade, and the sum is rounded toward zero once per light. Overlapping lights add. Nothing is capped at 255 while it is stored; the picture clamps the final colour.\n\n**Convert to shading** adds one light into the board's shading and removes the light. It is one edit, so one Undo brings the editable light back. Convert needs a board with at most one shading layer.\n\n## Seeing the result\n\nThe picture shows shading and lights together. **Draw tile shading** and **Draw lighting** hide either one in the picture only. Neither changes the board file.\n";
export const BITMAP_FONT_HELP_MARKDOWN = "# Drawing a bitmap font\n\nA bitmap font is the game's own lettering: 256 characters, each drawn as an 8 × 8 grid of square pixels. Open a `.fnt` file from the project, or choose **New font…** in the Files panel (in VS Code, **RPG Toolkit: New font**) to make one.\n\n## Choosing a character\n\nThe character strip shows all 256 slots in order: slot 0 is the top left, then across each row and down. A slot with a drawn glyph is bold. Click a slot, or use the arrow keys: Left and Right move one slot, Up and Down move 16, Home and End go to the ends of the row, and Ctrl+Home and Ctrl+End go to the first and last character. Choosing a slot changes nothing in the font.\n\n## Drawing a glyph\n\nThe glyph grid shows the selected slot as eight rows of eight pixels. The top left pixel is the first pixel of the first row, which is the order the file stores.\n\n- **Draw** (the default) adds ink; **Erase** removes it. Press on a pixel and drag to draw a line of them. The grid shows the stroke as you go and the whole stroke becomes one change when you let go, so one Undo takes it all back. Escape, or a stroke the pointer cancels, throws the unfinished stroke away.\n- From the keyboard, the grid is one stop: the arrow keys move the cursor, Space or Enter uses the current tool once, and Delete or Backspace erases the pixel under the cursor.\n- **Clear glyph** empties the selected character in one change.\n\nA stroke that changes nothing is not a change at all, so the font does not become unsaved for it.\n\n## Seeing it in a sample\n\nThe sample panel draws a line of text with the font as it is right now, saved or not. Type any sample, choose a size from 1 to 128 pixels, and switch **Bold**, **Italics** and **Underline** on and off. **Zoom** makes the picture bigger without smoothing it, so what you see are the pixels the game will draw.\n\nThese settings belong to the panel: they are never saved and never change the font. The text is laid out the way the game's `text()` lays it out — a line is one size tall — and it wraps at words when it grows past the panel.\n\nThe picture is drawn with the game's own rule. Each glyph pixel becomes a square, or a block when the size is bigger than 8. Bold adds a pixel to the right of every inked pixel, italics lean the glyph by moving each row to the right a little more than the row below it, and underline is a separate line under the text. Nothing is smoothed, so there are no grey edges.\n\n## Saving and using the font\n\nYour changes are ordinary edits: **Undo** and **Redo** in the glyph panel, or Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y from the grid, take a stroke or a keyboard change back and forward, and Save writes the file. Saving keeps every character you did not touch exactly as it was. A font file the editor cannot write back byte for byte is shown read-only with the reason, and is never converted.\n\nSave the font in the project's `Font` folder to use it in the game. Then `font(\"name\")` selects it by its file name, and `text()` draws with it, in the game and in a runner, the same way as the picture above.\n";
export const WHATS_NEW_MARKDOWN = "# What's New\n\n- Draw the game's own lettering in a bitmap font editor, open in the browser editor and in VS Code: choose among the 256 character slots, draw or erase an 8 × 8 glyph with the pointer or the keyboard (one stroke is one undoable edit), clear a glyph, and check a sample of text at any size from 1 to 128 pixels with bold, italics and underline at up to 4× zoom, drawn with the player's own bitmap rule and never saved with the font (#1939; [T-65](docs/editor-features.md#t-65-bitmap-font-editor), [bitmap font ink](docs/spec/engine/display-input-audio.md#reawakened-bitmap-font-ink)). Save keeps every character you did not touch and the file's own version, and a file the writer cannot reproduce opens read-only. **New font…** (Files panel) and **RPG Toolkit: New font** (VS Code) make an empty version 2.0 font in the project's `Font` folder. A `.fnt` saved there is now installed with the project's fonts, so `font(\"name\")` draws it in the game and in a runner, at any size, with bitmap metrics for measuring text ([project bitmap fonts](docs/spec/engine/display-input-audio.md#reawakened-project-bitmap-fonts)).\n\n- Paint shading and place lights on a board in the board editor: shade cells with Draw, Erase, Rectangle, Flood fill and Dropper (with the keyboard too), add spotlights and gradients, move their nodes by dragging or by number, convert a light to shading in one undoable step, and hide shading or lights in the picture. Lights now render as the amended spec describes (rotatable spotlights, clipped gradients as a band, cells tested at their top-left corner), so the S-LIGHT reference pictures and the village example picture changed (#1737, #3356).\n\n- The desktop CI lane stops each Electron child with a bounded SIGTERM/SIGKILL on both the success and timeout paths, retries only a dead GPU (forced SwiftShader, bounded backoff) and names a persistent GPU failure as infrastructure instead of reporting a bare shader message. A lost WebGL2 context raises `RenderContextLostError`, and the desktop job and its long steps have timeout backstops (#2169; [the five cold-start cases](docs/playing-the-desktop-player.md#the-five-cold-start-cases)).\n\n- The mobile lane's APK build is now a required job with classified Gradle retries that retry a 429/5xx/timeout and Gradle's ambiguous post-retry “Could not find…” output but never a stated 40x or compile failure, a warm dependency cache, and a required check that an emulator lane which started reports a verdict; a lane that never started is annotated, and only the emulator proof itself is advisory (#2721; [the Android proof](CONTRIBUTING.md#run-the-android-proof)).\n\n- `playergetpath` and `itemgetpath` reject a wrong argument count with a message that names exactly the counts they accept, one or four, instead of the VM's generic \"1 to 4\" range that advertised two counts the commands always refuse; every other command keeps the generic contiguous-range wording. Zero, two, three and five or more arguments are now proven at the VM boundary, and both accepted forms run through the VM rather than only a direct handler call (#3335; [R-69](docs/spec/engine/rpgcode-functions.md#r-69), [ERR-2](docs/spec/engine/rpgcode-errors-and-threads.md#err-2)).\n\n- Every item built-in that takes a handle and its player-side equivalents (`itemlocation`, `itemstep`, `itempath`, `itemdirection`, `itemspeed`, `itemstance`, `pushitem`, `wander`, `eraseitem`, `itemgetpath`; `playerlocation`, `playerstep`, `playerpath`, `playerdirection`, `playerspeed`, `playerstance`, `push`, `playergetpath`) now resolve the calling thread's `\"source\"` and `\"target\"` to the sprite those slots name, including the actor-id string an item use, equip or remove program stores, and the resolution also covers the effect each command queues. A numeric actor id resolves only the sprite linked to that actor, never a sprite id, handle or index, so the #3328 regression — `wander(\"target\")` stepping an unrelated item whose number equalled the actor id — cannot recur; an unresolved context keeps the command's dry no-op or located wrong-type error (#3331; [E-94](docs/spec/engine/rpgcode-functions.md#e-94), [E-95](docs/spec/engine/plugins-and-battle.md#e-95), [THR-2](docs/spec/engine/rpgcode-errors-and-threads.md#thr-2)).\n\n- Walk off a linked board edge in tile movement and the destination is checked before anyone moves: the board is loaded for checking only, the opposite-edge arrival is tested for bounds, layer count and exact-solid board vectors, and the destination's cancelable `beforeEnter` runs. A refusal consumes the refused step's own queue entry and keeps every later entry, leaves the player exactly where it stood, and blocks the step without counting it (no step program, battle check or party step). An accepted crossing is a placement, not a completed step, and the player faces the edge it crossed for direction- and point-style steps. The selected player alone is compared with the board extent, after the vector test, and a no-link edge is solid. The movement oracle edge scenes were re-recorded to the ruling (#1281, #1290; [R-66](docs/spec/engine/movement-and-collision.md#r-66), [E-138](docs/spec/engine/movement-and-collision.md#e-138)).\n\n- Edit a native tile’s collision and a pack asset’s walk-behind in their source files, then place, move, copy or remove linked packs in the native scene editor in the browser or VS Code (#1852; [native scene authoring](docs/editor-features.md#edit-reusable-collision-and-place-pack-assets-story-1217)). A narrow fence can leave the rest of its tile walkable. The player draws behind pack artwork inside its walk-behind region without covering transparent gaps (#3312).\n\n- Tile movement prechecks a direction step's whole destination before any advance and stops at the last safe position when an obstacle appears mid-step; the controlled player's blocked step is consumed and counted for end-of-step processing at its intended destination while other actors keep and retry the entry, queued directions are stored as relative intent from where the actor actually stands while point and waypoint targets stay absolute, pre-v4 saves migrate expanded direction targets, and `playergetpath`/`itemgetpath` report each queued entry's computed destination, including direction entries. The movement oracle fixtures were re-recorded to the rulings (#1281; [R-63](docs/spec/engine/movement-and-collision.md#r-63) to [R-65](docs/spec/engine/movement-and-collision.md#r-65), [ADR 0040](docs/adr/0040-tile-step-precheck-queues-and-linked-edge-preflight.md)).\n\n- Blocked movement now matches the original engine where the specification left a choice open: a counted blocked step feeds repeat-distance programs at the attempted tile (so a repeat program can re-fire once per blocked press), one live-blocking-thread test governs end-of-step processing for completed and blocked steps while thread-driven movement counts as free-roaming and pathfinding counts once per step, and `playergetpath`/`itemgetpath` report the queue count including the active step and write half-up whole pixels without touching the variables for a missing index (#1281, #3315; [R-67](docs/spec/engine/movement-and-collision.md#r-67) to [R-69](docs/spec/engine/rpgcode-functions.md#r-69)).\n\n- `playergetpath` and `itemgetpath` accept exactly one or exactly four arguments: both forms return the queue count including the step in progress, a missing index writes nothing and raises nothing, a copied waypoint counts once per cycle while a linked one is never counted, and an unresolved handle reports 3.1's `player not found` text. The movement plugin is now checked against its specification rows like every other documented plugin (#3315; [R-69](docs/spec/engine/rpgcode-functions.md#r-69)).\n\n- `setbutton` now draws its magenta-keyed image while keeping the same numeric slot independently clickable; replacing or clearing a slot updates both records together, and unavailable images draw nothing. `checkbutton(x, y, &slot)` now accepts the legacy third output argument, writing the hit slot or 0 without changing its 1/0 return (#2153; [button contract](docs/spec/engine/rpgcode-functions.md#reawakened-setbutton-images)).\n\n[Read the full changelog](https://github.com/tk-reawakened/rpgtoolkit-reawakened/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md).";
